    "finish_elapsed_s": 9.2123280360247,
    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat from your food and air, then evaporates into a gas. A compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils behind or beneath the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows through a valve that makes it cold again, ready to repeat the cycle. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays chilly—around 3°C—so bacteria grow slowly and food lasts longer."
  }
